Improvements for next run
I played the 5 day option but I went to day 6 with continued play. 5 days should be 5-days max and there should only be 2 scenarios a day for both game play modes
Player mode should not show what number roll they got to determine the difficulty of the mini game
There should be a brief explanation to tell the player why they were injured
There needs to be some incentive to complete the extra task
If the player was injured by an infected, there should be some opportunity to treat the wound within the next 3 turns
If the player has maxed out the amount of times they can syphon gas, that option should not even show up for them anymore not even greyed out. maybe another scenario for when the player runs out of fuel and has maxed out the number of times they can syphone gas
Make choices feel less hidden. Many effects only show up later, so players can’t tell why they died. A short dashboard flash after each choice would help, like “−10 water, +1 h, injury dodged.”
Callback scenes could say which earlier choice triggered them (“Because you helped the couple on Day 2…”).
Make passengers worth having. Right now they mostly cost food and water. Each one could also bring a perk while they ride, such as a mechanic who repairs the car, a nurse who heals, or a lookout who lowers the danger. Then picking someone up is a real trade-off instead of a pure act of kindness. But they may also add nothing it can be a random roll
Player has an option to replay a mini game if they lost (player can play each mini game a maximum of 2 times)
Add replay hooks.
Unlockable starting perks after a win, like an extra gallon or the coat.
A route-completion map on the title screen showing which cities you’ve beaten in each mode.
Badges for different endings, since there are already 7 endings and victory types.
Side missions could get the four-choice treatment, for example “Take it on,” “Not this trip,” “Take it, but ask for supplies up front,” and “Point them to someone else.”
Add a short how-to-play card the first time someone plays.
Add sound cues for low supplies, like a heartbeat when health is under 25%.
Add a “what if” replay at the end. On the end screen, show the player’s two or three biggest turning points and what the odds would have been if they’d picked differently. It uses the same random seed, so it’s a fair comparison. It teaches cause and effect, and it gets people to play again.
Dodge the wrecks minigame needs to be smaller. I have to scroll down mid-game to actually see the whole page.
Minigames need to be relevant to the player's surrounding choices. I had to do the black-ice and radio scramble minigames while I was at the safe zone trying to get in.
add in the group option on the menu with a sliding bar. default number of players is 1 but a player can go up to 5
menu seems to be a little busy so if we can condense some things like a small settings gear at the top right which houses the developer mode (still requiring the password that has been used in other game runs), music, and sound effect controls
at no point should the player have to scroll down in the game to see what they are doing in the mini game
take out the clock when showing player status update after selecting a choice
add back the brief descriptions of why health, food, water, or fuel went up or down.
During the game: make points visible all the time
A score counter on the dashboard. Points count up live, with a little pop and sound for big moments, like “+60 Helped the farmer” or “+100 Made the deadline with 6 hours to spare.”
Streak bonuses. Three smart choices in a row earns ×1.5 on the next bonus, and a bad choice breaks the streak. That gives people a reason to think instead of tapping fast.
Risk pays more. Risky choices that work out pay extra, and Hard mode multiplies the final score by 1.5. Bold players can climb the board even if they barely survive.
Speed bonus. Answering in under 10 seconds of the 20-second timer adds a few points. In a live classroom that creates pressure and noise.
Mini game points. A perfect lock pick or siphon earns points, so the arcade parts count toward the leaderboard too.
Score breakdown animation. Each part counts up one at a time, something like “Survived +1,000 · Health +180 · Kindness +240 · Speed +90 · Streaks +150 = 1,660.” That’s the dopamine hit.
A title based on how you played, such as “The Good Samaritan” (most helping), “Speed Demon” (most time to spare), “Barely Breathing” (survived under 10% health), or “Lone Wolf” (never picked anyone up).
Name entry for the top 10. If you place, you type a nickname of up to 12 characters. I’d suggest nicknames rather than real names, since the board is on a shared screen and the link can be shared further. A simple filter can block inappropriate words.
Rank reveal: “You placed #3 of 27 players today.”
The presenter scoreboard in the developer mode ONLY
A separate “scoreboard” screen you open on the projector. It refreshes every few seconds as people finish, and new entries slide in with a sound.
Class session code. You start a session (for example “ACC380”) and players enter it, so the board shows only your class from that day, not every past run.
Tabs: top scores, plus fun categories like “Most people helped,” “Shortest survival,” and “How people died” (a live bar chart of cold vs. fuel vs. infection, which is another nice analytics moment).
A final “winner” screen with confetti to close the presentation.
